---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditOverlayEditor from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditOverlayEditor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sign-pdf">
  <PdfEditOverlayEditor client:load mode="sign" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Sign a PDF without sending it anywhere</h2>
      <p>
        The documents you sign are often the ones you least want to upload: an employment contract, an
        NDA, a tenancy agreement, a power of attorney, a settlement.
      </p>
      <p>
        This browser tab opens the PDF, draws your signature on a canvas and assembles the signed copy.
        There is no server the file could go to. To confirm, open your network inspector, or disconnect
        from the internet before you start.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Three ways to make a signature</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Draw</strong> gives the best result on a touchscreen or with a stylus. Sign large: the
          drawing is trimmed to the ink and scaled down when placed. A large signature reduced looks sure;
          a small one enlarged looks unsteady.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Type</strong> writes your name in a handwriting font already installed on your system,
          then converts it to a transparent image. It is quick, and the better option if you only have a
          mouse.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Upload</strong>: sign a white sheet of paper with a dark pen, photograph it in good
          light, and drop the photo in. Leave "make the paper transparent" on. It fades near-white pixels,
          so only the ink goes onto the PDF.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The signature exists only in this tab's memory. It is not saved to a library or to local storage,
        and it is gone when you close the tab.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Visual signature or digital signature?</h2>
      <p>
        These are different things, but people often use the two terms for the same thing.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[640px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Visual signature (this tool)</th>
              <th scope="col">Digital signature (PAdES, eIDAS, DSC)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>What it is</td>
              <td>An image of your signature drawn onto the page</td>
              <td>A cryptographic hash of the document signed with your private key</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Proves</td>
              <td>Intent to sign</td>
              <td>Identity, plus that the file has not changed since signing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Needs</td>
              <td>Nothing</td>
              <td>A certificate from a certificate authority, and usually a timestamp service</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Accepted for</td>
              <td>Most contracts, forms, consents and letters</td>
              <td>Court filings, tender submissions, some tax and land registries</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Possible in a browser with no server</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No: there is no certificate store and no timestamp authority</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Under the US ESIGN Act, the EU's eIDAS regulation and the UK's Electronic Communications Act, an
        electronic signature is generally valid where the signer intended to sign. A drawn or typed
        signature on the document meets that test. If the other party asks for a certificate-based
        signature, you need desktop signing software with a certificate token, or a service that issues
        certificates. This is not legal advice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Placing the signature</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Go to the signature page before you create the signature. The tool places it on the page you are looking at.</li>
        <li>Drag it so its baseline sits on the printed line and slightly overhangs the ends. A signature that fits neatly inside a box looks printed.</li>
        <li>Make it about two-thirds the width of the line. A signature that fills the whole line is usually too big.</li>
        <li>Add the date with the <strong>Text</strong> tool in the date field, and your initials on each page that asks for them.</li>
        <li>Apply, download, and check the result in a PDF reader before you send it.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Signing a form and keeping it fillable</h2>
      <p>
        The signature is page content and does not change the form dictionary, so an AcroForm PDF stays
        fillable after signing. To stop others from changing the answers, run the signed file through
        <a href="/flatten-pdf">Flatten PDF</a>, which merges the fields into the page. To fill the form
        first, use <a href="/fill-pdf-form">Fill Out PDF Forms</a>. If it has no fields, type onto it with
        <a href="/add-text-to-pdf">Add Text to PDF</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
